E
"either"
"Welcome to The Forests Edge.


Whether you're an experienced mudder or new to the game The Forests Edge (TFE)

can take a bit of learning to understand how to interact with the game and its

players.  This guide is intended to help you through the steps of learning to

play here.


The first thing you should know about are the help files.

There are a lot of files and reading through them can be quite confusing but

they're there to help.  Don't be afraid to ask others for help if you need it

to figure things out.  The first help file that will be of use is the newbie

help files.  You can read it by typing @bhelp newbie@n.


Help newbie will give you an overview of all the things you can do in the game

and how to interact with the game and other players.  Additionally this guide

has some other sections you can look at to help with your first steps as a

player.  You can type @blook guide index@n to see what else you can read in

this guide.

"
E
"index"
"Index for the newbie help guide

-------------------------------


You can type @blook guide@n followed by any of the following categories:


@bSuggestions@n - for suggestions on what to do first

@bTips@n     - for those little extras that are nice to know


@bWarriors@n - for warriors, monks and thieves

@bCasters@n  - for mages and clerics

@bHybrids@n  - for bards, rangers and paladins


@bhunting@n - For tips on hunting your newbie area

"
E
"Suggestions"
"Every character starts off in a room that normally has food and drink on the

floor.  You can type @bget all@n to pick it all up at once or you can pick

each piece up individually.  Its a good idea to get all the food and drink

there to begin with.  Come back periodically as more appears there from time

to help them out.  You can eat and drink with @geat apple@n and @gdrink cup@n

where apple and cup are replaced by the items you have picked up.


Before you go hunting, you'll likely want to spend some of your practice

points on improving skills.  See @bhelp practice points@n and @bhelp

practice@n for more information on these.  What you'll want to spend these

practice points on first depends largely on what class (fighter, mage, etc)

you are.


Every starting town has an altar in it.  You should find the altar and

sacrifice something to the diety of your choice.  You can pray for assistance

once you've sacrificed to and follow a diety.  See also @bhelp religion@n,

@bhelp sacrifice@n, and @bhelp pray.@n

"
E
"tips"
"Most commands can be shortened quite a bit.  You can type @blook guide index@n

or you can shorten it down to just @bl gui ind@n.  Experimentation will show

how little you need to type but learning what you can shorten helps a lot.

(particularly during combat)


Read through @bhelp wimpy@n and ask for help if you need it.  Wimpy settings

help you automatically flee from combat or pray for help if things are going

too badly.  Its well worth learning to understand.


Dont be afraid to ask for help if you're confused.  We have a lot of players

quite willing to help explain things.  Type @bnewbie @gtext@n where @gtext@n is

replaced by what you want to ask and it will broadcast it to those players who

are listening to help out with such things.  There are other @bchannels@n and its

worth learning which are for what as some people are touchy about keeping the

in-character role playing channels seperate from out-of-character chatter.


@blook guide tips2@n for some futher tips.

"
E
"warriors"
"I'm using the term warrior kind of loosely here to define the non-casting

classes.  Most of them will start off largely the same.  The first thing

you'll want to spend practice points on is probably 1 level of climb and 1

level of swimming.  The rest you can put into the weapon skill for whatever

starting weapon you have and that will help you get through your first few

levels a lot more readily.


Look for trainers in town.  Players can't train you, only Non-Player

Characters (NPCs) sometimes called Mobs can train you.  Walk up to any

character and type @bpractice@n to see if they can train you in anything. 

You'll either get a message saying there is no one there who can train you or

a list of what you can train in.  You can type @bpractice swimming@n once to

practice one point into swimming.


Once you're done practicing skills you should find the nearest newbie area and

hunt small things there.  Find a safe place and @bsleep@n between fights to

heal up faster.  If you @bflee@n from a fight whatever you were fighting will

still be ready to kill you so don't sleep anywhere near it.

"
E
"casters"
"Even though you're a caster, you should read through the @bwarriors@n section

of this guide so you'll know about @bpractices@n and skills.


To cast a spell, you need to have at least one level of skill in it.  Typing

@babilities spell@n will show what you can currently learn and how many skill

levels you have in each so far.  Anything with a level listed as @gunk@n means

you haven't learned it yet.  You'll need to find an trainer to gain your first

level.  After learning 1 level in a skill you can use a trainer to learn more


To begin with you should already have some starting levels in the spells

you'll need.  You'll likely want to practice a level of climb, a level of

swimming and then either more points in magic missile for mages, healing for

cleric, or quite possibly your starting weapon skill.  I'd personally suggest

upping your weapon skill as your starting fights will rely more on that

ability then spell ability and your spells will go up comparatively faster on

their own than your weapon skill will.


For more magic info, @blook guide casters2@n

"
E
"casters2"
"Some spells require reagents and some dont.

Create Light doesn't need a reagent.  Type @bcast create light@n and you'll

create a ball of light you can use to see at night.  Eventually the ball of

light will wear out and you'll need to create another.

Magic Missile and Armor require reagents and you'll need to acquire more each

time you want to cast it.  You'll start with a few reagents for spells you

know so you can cast them to begin with.


Two other facets of magic you'll need to learn about are @bprepare@n and

@bleech@n.  There are help files for each.  Mages will need to @bprepare@n a

magic missile before casting and Clerics will need to @bprepare@n a cure light

healing spell before casting it.

Clerics will need to understand @bleech@n right away to effectively use energy

with regards to their armor spell but mages will need to learn about it in

time with some spells as well later on.

"
E
"hybrids"
"Hybrid classes are those classes that have a mix of fighting and casting

skills.  In the case of Rangers and Paladins you won't start getting spells

until level 3.  In the case of bards, you'll start with a few spells so you'll

need to understand magic right away.


I suggest you read through both the warrior and caster section as you'll need

to understand how both classes work to understand your own class. 

Additionally, its suggested you learn a level of climb skill, a level of

swimming and then probably put most of the rest into your starting weapon

skill.

"
E
"hunting"
"Each starting town has a newbie hunting ground where you can go begin learning

to fight and use your skills.  If you started in:


@bChiiron@n - head outside the east, west or north gates and hunt rabbits

@bVoaleth@n - head into the eastern caves and hunt worms and bats

@bKha-da@n - find the goblin cave south of Kha-da and hunt goblins

@bVaasa@n - hunt beetles in the lembas farm below and north of town

@bEnt'Moot@n - head east from town and south a ways

           you'll need to hunt beetles in the elven vaasa farm


You'll want to type @bget all corpse@n when you kill something and then @bskin

corpse@n after that.  This will get any items the thing you killed had then

see if you can skin the creature for pelts and such items of use.  You can

sell these things to certain shops back in town for money which you'll need to

buy more gear and train in new skills

"
E
"tips2"
"There are several settings you can change to make things easier for you.

For example typing @boptions@n will show you several settings you currently

have on and off.

If you have @GAutoloot@n off, it will have a @G-@n for its setting.  If its

set to @G1@n you will automatically loot coins off things you kill.  If its

set to @G3@n you will also take any objects it was carrying from the corpse

saving you the trouble of typing @Gget all corpse@n after a fight.


See @bhelp options@n for what each option does.

See also @bhelp iflags@n and @bhelp messages@n for related info.


The various chat channels and noteboards will be useful for communication.

@bhelp channels@n and @bhelp note@n will provide info on those.

"
P
-1

